### What is the Single Responsibility Principle?

SRP says a class should change for exactly one reason. A class that validates a transfer, computes fees, saves to the database, and sends notifications changes for four different reasons. A change to the SMS provider risks breaking the validation logic.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// VIOLATION: four reasons to change in one class
public class TransferManagerBad {
    public void execute(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        // 1. Validate accounts
        if (from == null || to == null) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid accounts");
        // 2. Compute fee
        BigDecimal fee = amount.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.001"));
        // 3. Persist to database
        System.out.println("Saving transfer: " + amount + " fee: " + fee);
        // 4. Send SMS notification
        System.out.println("SMS to " + to + ": You received " + amount);
    }
}
```

The fix splits into focused classes:

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

public class TransferValidator {
    public void validate(String from, String to) {
        if (from == null || to == null) throw new IllegalArgumentException("Invalid accounts");
    }
}

public class FeeCalculator {
    public BigDecimal compute(BigDecimal amount) {
        return amount.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.001"));
    }
}

public class TransferRepository {
    public void save(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount, BigDecimal fee) {
        System.out.println("Saved transfer: " + amount + " fee: " + fee);
    }
}

public class NotificationService {
    public void notify(String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        System.out.println("SMS to " + to + ": You received " + amount);
    }
}
```

Each class now has one reason to change.

### What is the Open/Closed Principle?

OCP says you add new behaviour by adding new code, not by editing tested code. A fee calculator with a growing if-else on trade type must be edited every time a new type appears, risking existing branches.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// VIOLATION: every new trade type edits this method
public class TradeFeeCalculatorBad {
    public BigDecimal calculate(String type, BigDecimal amount) {
        if ("EQUITY".equals(type)) return amount.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.001"));
        else if ("FX".equals(type)) return amount.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.0005"));
        // adding DERIVATIVE means editing here
        return BigDecimal.ZERO;
    }
}

// FIX: strategy interface
public interface FeeStrategy {
    BigDecimal calculate(BigDecimal amount);
}

public class EquityFee implements FeeStrategy {
    public BigDecimal calculate(BigDecimal amount) {
        return amount.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.001"));
    }
}

public class FxFee implements FeeStrategy {
    public BigDecimal calculate(BigDecimal amount) {
        return amount.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.0005"));
    }
}

// Adding DerivativeFee is a new class – no edits to existing code
public class DerivativeFee implements FeeStrategy {
    public BigDecimal calculate(BigDecimal amount) {
        return amount.multiply(new BigDecimal("0.002"));
    }
}
```

### What is the Liskov Substitution Principle?

LSP says a subtype must honour the contract of its supertype. The classic violation is `Square extends Rectangle`: a Rectangle lets you set width and height independently, but Square overrides the setters to keep them equal.

```java
package com.bank.model;

public class Rectangle {
    protected int width;
    protected int height;

    public void setWidth(int w) { this.width = w; }
    public void setHeight(int h) { this.height = h; }
    public int area() { return width * height; }
}

// VIOLATION: overriding setters breaks the Rectangle contract
public class Square extends Rectangle {
    @Override
    public void setWidth(int w) { this.width = w; this.height = w; }
    @Override
    public void setHeight(int h) { this.width = h; this.height = h; }
}

// Caller expects Rectangle behaviour:
// rect.setWidth(5); rect.setHeight(10); assert rect.area() == 50;
// But Square gives area = 100!
```

The fix is to not model Square as a subtype of Rectangle. Use a common `Shape` abstraction with an `area()` method instead.

```java
public interface Shape {
    int area();
}

public class RectangleFixed implements Shape {
    private final int width, height;
    public RectangleFixed(int w, int h) { this.width = w; this.height = h; }
    public int area() { return width * height; }
}

public class SquareFixed implements Shape {
    private final int side;
    public SquareFixed(int s) { this.side = s; }
    public int area() { return side * side; }
}
```

### What is the Interface Segregation Principle?

ISP says do not force a class to implement methods it does not use. A fat `Animal` interface forces `Dog` to throw on `fly()`.

```java
package com.bank.model;

// VIOLATION: fat interface
public interface Animal {
    void eat();
    void sleep();
    void fly();
    void swim();
}

public class Dog implements Animal {
    public void eat() { System.out.println("Dog eats"); }
    public void sleep() { System.out.println("Dog sleeps"); }
    public void fly() { throw new UnsupportedOperationException("Dogs cannot fly"); }
    public void swim() { System.out.println("Dog swims"); }
}

// FIX: small role interfaces
public interface Eater { void eat(); }
public interface Swimmer { void swim(); }
public interface Flyer { void fly(); }

public class DogFixed implements Eater, Swimmer {
    public void eat() { System.out.println("Dog eats"); }
    public void swim() { System.out.println("Dog swims"); }
}
```

In banking, a fat `Account` interface with `calculateInterest`, `checkOverdraft`, and `tradeStocks` forces a plain savings account to stub out trading. Split into `InterestBearing`, `Overdraftable`, and `Tradable`.

### What is the Dependency Inversion Principle?

DIP says high-level policy should depend on an abstraction, and the concrete detail should be injected. A `TransferService` that does `new EmailNotifier()` inside itself is welded to email and is hard to test.

```java
package com.bank.service;

import java.math.BigDecimal;

// VIOLATION: welded to EmailNotifier
public class TransferServiceBad {
    private final EmailNotifier notifier = new EmailNotifier();

    public void transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        System.out.println("Transferred " + amount);
        notifier.send(to, "You received " + amount);
    }
}

// FIX: depend on abstraction, inject implementation
public interface Notifier {
    void send(String to, String message);
}

public class EmailNotifier implements Notifier {
    public void send(String to, String message) {
        System.out.println("Email to " + to + ": " + message);
    }
}

public class SmsNotifier implements Notifier {
    public void send(String to, String message) {
        System.out.println("SMS to " + to + ": " + message);
    }
}

public class TransferServiceFixed {
    private final Notifier notifier;

    public TransferServiceFixed(Notifier notifier) {
        this.notifier = notifier;
    }

    public void transfer(String from, String to, BigDecimal amount) {
        System.out.println("Transferred " + amount);
        notifier.send(to, "You received " + amount);
    }
}
```

Now you can inject a mock in tests or an SMS notifier in production without touching the service.

### How do SOLID principles reinforce each other?

These principles work as a system. DIP plus OCP is what makes the Strategy pattern powerful: you depend on the strategy interface (DIP) and add new strategies without editing (OCP). ISP makes LSP easier because small contracts are easier to honour fully.

```java
package com.bank.demo;

import java.math.BigDecimal;
import java.util.Map;

public class SolidTogetherDemo {
    public static void main(String[] args) {
        // OCP + DIP: strategies selected at runtime
        Map<String, FeeStrategy> fees = Map.of(
            "EQUITY", new EquityFee(),
            "FX", new FxFee()
        );

        String tradeType = "EQUITY";
        BigDecimal amount = new BigDecimal("500000");
        BigDecimal fee = fees.get(tradeType).calculate(amount);
        System.out.println(tradeType + " fee: " + fee);

        // DIP: inject notifier
        TransferServiceFixed svc = new TransferServiceFixed(new SmsNotifier());
        svc.transfer("ACC-001", "ACC-002", new BigDecimal("25000"));
    }
}
```

```text
EQUITY fee: 500.000
Transferred 25000
SMS to ACC-002: You received 25000
```

### What are common mistakes when applying SOLID?

- Applying SRP too aggressively and creating dozens of one-line classes that are harder to navigate than the original.
- Creating interfaces for classes that will only ever have one implementation, adding indirection for no benefit.
- Confusing ISP with "one method per interface" – ISP is about role cohesion, not minimum size.
- Using DIP where no variation or testing seam is needed, creating pointless abstraction layers.

### What should a senior engineer know?

- SOLID principles are guidance, not rigid rules. Applied mechanically they create needless interfaces.
- The real skill is recognising when a principle earns its place: a second implementation, a growing switch, a hard-to-test dependency.
- In Spring, DIP is automatic through constructor injection, and OCP is natural with bean registries.
- In a microservice, SRP maps to bounded contexts: each service has one business reason to exist.
